<p>The modern launch is a fireworks display: a big noise, a brief light, and then the long dark. I’ve come to prefer a different approach. Open the doors quietly. Invite a few people in. Pay attention.</p>

<p>A slow launch is not an excuse for timidity. It is a way of listening before you amplify. It gives you room to fix the embarrassing parts while the audience is still small enough to forgive them. It also gives the product time to develop a point of view.</p>

<h2>The first hundred</h2>

<p>The first hundred users are not a market. They are collaborators. They will use your thing in ways you did not plan. They will ask for the feature you deliberately left out. They will tell you, with varying degrees of tact, what the product is actually for.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Launch with a clear promise, not a long feature list.</li>
  <li>Write to the first hundred people like they are individuals, because they are.</li>
  <li>Wait until the story is true before you tell it loudly.</li>
</ul>

<p>There is a time for scale. Eventually the doors should open wider. But the early days are a rare gift. You will never have so few people paying so much attention again.</p>

<p>Slow is not the opposite of ambitious. It is a way of being ambitious about the right thing.</p>
